 we have to look  hard does Jesus does who Jesus is make a  difference for Who I am  according to this passage if Jesus is a  miracle worker a teacher a kind and  moral man a lover of the poor and the  outcast if he is any and all of these  things and I worship Him and follow him  doesn't really change who I am  that fits with my perception of myself I  too can be a teacher I too can be a  moral person or a servant without  changing a lot about what I think about  my life or the world I'm still a normal  person but if Jesus says what he says  today and he tells me we're all going to  the cross all of us let's go he's headed  to the cross just like a common criminal  if he's headed towards suffering and  death and I still worship Him and follow  him it does change Who I am because I  can't be a normal good person if I'm  willingly following him to his death I  become a crazy person so welcome  crazy people  no sane person wants to suffer and die  if Jesus is a failure and a criminal and  so am I and so who he is becomes a very  important part of who we are and worse  yet he says if I want to follow him I  have to deny myself and take up my own  cross and follow him deny myself follow  him to the cross  doesn't sound like the path to happiness  and fulfillment doesn't it's funny how  we skip this part a lot of the time when  we're telling people about Jesus it's  all about fulfilment wholeness joy who  would be crazy enough to worship this  man Saint Peter wasn't responding  unreasonably when Jesus rebuked him I  would have done the same and I daresay  you would have done the same if I were  to get up here and say okay it's all  been great so far but I'm gonna suffer  and die and you should too I think I'd  be out of a job  I think Calista would call Lyn Delorme  er and report me I would have done the  same thing Peter did I would have said  of course you're not going to suffer and  die Jesus you're too nice people like  you and we've got so much invested in  you if you suffer and die then  everything we've given to you is worth  nothing Jesus is saying if you can't  stand where my path is headed then  you're following me for the wrong  reasons because the path I'm  trailblazing and that you're following  leads to losing your life and to  understand Jesus I think we need to ask  that question the second question which  life am i losing and which life am i  saving  he says we're gonna lose one and we're  gonna gain one which one is which which  part of our life really fits they're my  guests from everything Jesus says is  that I'm supposed to lose the life  that's based on what other people have  told me about what I should do and who I  should be it's the very modern life we  all lead and it I think it's part of  living in such an egalitarian society  and especially amongst high achievers as  many of you are in this room a life  that's based on some weird compromise  between what I need to do to keep other  people happy and we are people pleasers  and what I need to do to keep myself  occupied and sated not just keeping  others happy but also distracting them  from my flaws keeping them pleased with  the good things I do for them and in the  same way with myself keeping my focus  away from who I really am what my real  problems are and keeping myself happy  with the stories I tell myself about Who  I am the stories I tell myself that keep  a thin fog over everything I know that  sounds odd but isn't that the life most  of us lead the life stuck somewhere on  that fairly narrow spectrum between  trying to please other people and trying  to please myself how can I find a career  that I will enjoy that will still make  my parents happy and make me lots of  money how can i balance my relationships  and my work and still have time for  myself how can I make new friends at  Yale join ten clubs and take six classes  and get straight A's in all of them how  do I do that it's that fine line between  pleasing other people and pleasing  my