nd what I'm  good at I think that the life Jesus  calls us to lose is the life defined by  our passions and he asks us to save a  life defined by his passion one of my  favorite stories about how you know  you're in the right path is a funny  story you've heard me tell about Pablo  Casals  perhaps the greatest cellist ever it was  touring the United States in 1901  traveling across the nation and he was  supposed to do 80 different engagements  but halfway through the tour he was  climbing in the mountains near San  Francisco and someone up above him  kicked back a rock as he was sort of  clambering up the side of the mountain  and this rock came down and fell on his  hand and crushed his fingers pretty  rough for a cellist he said the first  thought that came to his mind when he  looked at his crumpled hand was thank  God I'll never have to play the cello  again at least at that moment early in  his life Casals didn't embrace his life  as a cellist as a path towards happiness  or pursuing a passion his life as a  cellist sounds like something between a  mania and a compulsion and the life  Jesus wants us to find is the life we  can't seem to avoid that life we feel  strangely compelled to follow not the  life of our own fulfillment or anyone  else's fulfillment  I think this different life is not about  success it's not about pleasing others  or even pleasing your idea of who you  think you are or who I think I am it is  about as my old friend Glenn Mitchell  has written gaining the freedom to be  fully present to life which has  everything to do with accepting our  death and our suffering  and letting go of our efforts to control  life Jesus invites us to let go of our  grip on life enough to create a holy  space for the gift of God's presence  here and now  to the old life of trying to meet  everyone's expectations rising again to  only his expectations for us to love and  sacrifice for others how do you know  what's the right path my rule of thumb  that it is whatever would hurt the most  to give it up whatever would sink  deepest into your definition of who you  are in relation to yourself and other  people because that's the one where all  the trouble is built in that's the life  that's hardest to let go of like  publicus all's I think the true / the  true life of purpose often feels more  like a compulsion than a choice it may  not be something you're good at how  could that be how could God call me to  something I'm not perfect at exactly  what God calls us to if you read your  Bible it may not be something that makes  me deeply happy but it's something we  can't not do because we can feel God's  Spirit leading us towards it the great  illusion of our time as Daniel Gilbert  the Harvard psychologist wrote in his  book stumbling on happiness is that we  think we know what will make us happy  the greatest delusion of all time  and we strive for it and strive for it  and we get it and we're still not happy  but we keep going after it and we get it  again and we're still not happy that  next boyfriend that next car their next  achievement the next job that's the one  that will make me happy right at our  deacons retreat last weekend a number of  the Deacons remarked that because the  church didn't place a lot of specific  demands on them when push came to shove  and other groups were desperate for help  and studies piled up Church got set  aside somebody else would do it  they showed real courage and honesty to  admit this and we all do it even mean  not just with this church but with a  whole call of Christ I think what we  need is sacrifice defined as the  dictionary does as for going something  valued for the sake of something having  a more pressing claim the gospel has  pressing claim on us than the stories we  tell ourselves about ourselves when we  answer the question who is Jesus and we  see in him a figure of triumphant glory  or social change or personal fulfillment  we end up defining ourselves by goals  that are less than ultimate we achieve  power or change or whatever circumstance  we thought would make us happy and we  end up thinking is that all there is but  if we take up the cross he gives us a  note this is a very important point it's  not the cross we choose a cross that  someone else chooses for us if we take  up the cross he gives us our lives  become not successful or even thrilling  or even blissful but they become holy  which is more lasting than any of those  things our lives become our own way our  lives become our own in a way the lives  we would choose for ourselves could  never be and we can welcome our true  selves without fear and love others  without judgment if he's going to the  cross we go with him what we lose is  nothing to regret what we lose is just  busy work to justify ourselves to  ourselves and to others what we gain is  the simple freedom of following him
 

